---
/**
 * About: the public biography. Facts come from copy the owner has published: the 2026-08 founder
 * note on hllmr.com and the owner's LinkedIn profile (supplied 2026-10-18 as starting content, with
 * leave to expand). A typographic plate stands in for the portrait until the owner's photo arrives
 * (owner decision 2026-10-18).
 */
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHead from "../../components/PageHead.astro";
import Entry from "../../components/Entry.astro";
import Plate from "../../components/Plate.astro";
import { ENVIRONMENTS } from "../../lib/cv";
import { HUB, LINKS, LOCALITY, NAME, PROFILES } from "../../lib/site";

const linkedin = PROFILES.find((p) => p.label === "LinkedIn");
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`About · ${NAME}`}
  description="David Hellmer builds engineering capability for physical systems: twenty years in building automation and low-voltage systems, businesses built and run, and now applied AI and software."
  ogType="profile"
>
  <PageHead k="About" title="About" lede="I build control systems, and lately I build software the way I was trained to build control systems." />

  <div class="log wrap">
    <Entry no="01" label="At a glance">
      <div class="split">
        <Plate caption="David Hellmer · Dallas–Fort Worth" />
        <dl class="facts">
          <dt>Name</dt>
          <dd>{NAME}</dd>
          <dt>Based</dt>
          <dd>{LOCALITY}</dd>
          <dt>Work</dt>
          <dd>
            Founder &amp; Principal Engineer, <a href={HUB}>HLLMR Ventures</a>; Senior Applications Engineer, Building
            Automation Systems, Enviromatic Systems
          </dd>
          <dt>Trained</dt>
          <dd>U.S. Navy Nuclear Power Training Command</dd>
          <dt>Field</dt>
          <dd>Building automation, low-voltage and OT systems; applied AI and software</dd>
          <dt>Off hours</dt>
          <dd>DJ, for thirty years</dd>
          <dt>Elsewhere</dt>
          <dd>{linkedin?.href ? <a href={linkedin.href}>{linkedin.handle}</a> : null} · <a href="/elsewhere/">more</a></dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </Entry>

    <Entry no="02" label="The short version" id="bio">
      <h2 id="bio">I build engineering capability.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The people, standards, software and delivery practices that help teams solve hard problems in physical systems.
          My career spans more than twenty years across building automation, low-voltage systems, mission-critical
          facilities and business ownership. I have worked every part of it: field installation and programming, project
          management, engineering, estimating, sales support and department leadership.
        </p>
        <p>
          It started in the Navy's nuclear power program, where you assume nothing works until you have watched it work.
          Then security and fire systems, then building automation, commissioning and the engineering behind it. I have
          established and led a building automation division, and built a manufacturing business to national distribution,
          more than $1M a year and a team of twelve.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Entry>

    <Entry no="03" label="Where" id="where">
      <h2 id="where">Buildings where failure is not an option.</h2>
      <ul class="env">{ENVIRONMENTS.map((e) => <li>{e}</li>)}</ul>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Those environments taught me to define the expected behaviour, verify the actual system and keep the evidence of
          what happened: the commissioning discipline that proves a building does what the drawings say.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Entry>

    <Entry no="04" label="Now" id="now">
      <h2 id="now">Applied AI, with a commissioning engineer's distrust.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          I build software with AI coding agents every day, and the same discipline turns out to be the missing piece. An
          agent will report a task complete when it is not. A control nobody has watched deny anything is an assumption. A
          plan regenerated from the code launders drift into the record. None of that is new. It is the failure mode every
          commissioning engineer already knows, arriving in an industry that has not learned to distrust a status point yet.
        </p>
        <p>
          At Enviromatic Systems I combine controls engineering with internal software: tools that went from one manager's
          request to department-wide use, an operations platform, and the department's Applications Engineer curriculum.
          Through <a href={HUB}>HLLMR Ventures</a> I build products of my own (Writwall, stratum-dsp and Silo among them),
          owning the engineering decisions, integration, verification and delivery.
        </p>
      </div>
      <a class="more" href="/cv/">The full CV</a>
    </Entry>

    <Entry no="05" label="Next" id="next">
      <h2 id="next">What I want to do next</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Lead engineering and applied-AI work where building-systems expertise shapes better products and better
          operations: BAS and OT engineering leadership, and forward-deployed work with real ownership of customer outcomes
          and product direction. The people who design, deliver and operate buildings deserve better information, better
          tools and more reliable processes.
        </p>
      </div>
      <a class="more" href="/elsewhere/">Get in touch</a>
    </Entry>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.9fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr);
    gap: var(--space-6);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .env {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-5);
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 8px;
  }
  .env li {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    padding: 5px 10px;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    background: var(--surface);
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .split {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
